        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Drawn here rather than reposted. The joke has been going around
          without an author attached to it, and it is too good to pass on
          unsigned.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 01 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">01 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; Above and below</p>
      <h2>The part that gets demoed is the part above the water.</h2>
      <p>
        A launch video shows the agent answering a question in plain English.
        It does not show which table the answer came from, which rows were
        excluded, or who decided that a canceled trial still counts as a
        customer. Those choices are underwater, and they are the ones that
        decide whether the answer holds up in week six.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both buildings in the drawing were finished. Only one of them was
        photographed. The difference between them is not ambition or taste. It
        is whether somebody drove the piles down to rock before the floors went
        up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 02 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">02 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The rented part</p>
      <h2>The model is the one thing your competitor can copy this afternoon.</h2>
      <p>
        Frontier models come from a handful of shops, and switching between
        them is a config change. Whatever advantage a team holds because of
        which model it picked has a shelf life measured in months.
      </p>
      <p>
        What nobody can copy quickly is a definition of <em>active
        subscriber</em> that survived three arguments with finance, a join
        graph that has been checked for fan-out, and a written record of which
        tables are safe to trust before 9am. That is the context an agent runs
        on, and it is slow, unglamorous, local work.
      </p>
      <p class="gnd-note">
        An agentic workflow is a loop of tool calls against your systems. Each
        call is only as good as the context it was handed. The loop does not
        improve bad context, it repeats it faster and in a more convincing tone
        of voice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 03 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">03 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The pile schedule</p>
      <h2>What &ldquo;quality context&rdquo; means when you have to build it.</h2>
      <p>
        Context is a vague word, which is why it survives so many strategy
        decks. Broken into piles, it is a short and very concrete list.
      </p>
      <ol class="gnd-spec">
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Meaning</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >What the business calls a subscriber, a churn, a month. Including
            the exclusions, which is where the arguments live.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Grain</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >The level each fact table is stored at. Get it wrong and every
            answer above it is plausible and mathematically false.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Joins</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >Which paths are valid and which duplicate revenue across marketing
            events. An agent will happily take either.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Freshness</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >When each table last landed, exposed to the agent rather than
            buried in an orchestration log.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Grants</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >Who the agent is acting for, and what that person is allowed to
            see. A helpful agent with a service account is a data leak with
            good manners.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Provenance</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >Which of the above a human has actually reviewed, and when. The
            pile you never inspected is the one that snapped.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="gnd-note">
        None of these are model problems, and none of them get better when the
        next model ships.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 04 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">04 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The silt</p>
      <h2>An agent let loose on a spaghetti warehouse fails the way people do, only faster.</h2>
      <p>
        Point an agent at a warehouse with no tested pipelines, no
        documentation, no separation between staging, intermediate, and mart
        models, no dimensional modeling worth the name, and no record of why
        anything was built the way it was. It will join whatever looks
        joinable. So does every analyst who has ever been handed that
        warehouse. The agent is not making a new class of mistake, it is
        making the usual one at machine speed and in a confident paragraph.
      </p>
      <p>
        Analytics engineering is the pile driving. Tests that fail the build
        when a key stops being unique. A staging layer that renames and casts
        once, so nobody downstream invents a second version of the truth. Mart
        models shaped as facts and dimensions, at a stated grain, so a
        question about products cannot quietly be answered at order grain.
      </p>
      <p class="gnd-note">
        And the decision records. Most of what an agent needs to know is not
        which model exists but why it was built that way: why revenue excludes
        credits, why the trial table is snapshotted nightly, which trade-off
        was accepted and what it costs. An architecture decision record is
        context written down at the moment the judgment was made, which is the
        only moment anyone remembers it.
      </p>
      <p>
        No amount of prompt engineering substitutes for that work. A retrieval
        step over bad models retrieves bad models.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 05 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">05 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The new failure mode</p>
      <h2>Bad data used to look wrong. Now it reads well.</h2>
      <p>
        A dashboard with a broken join shows a number that somebody in the room
        finds odd. That person is the safety net, and the net has been holding
        for twenty years of BI.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent with the same broken join writes a fluent paragraph, cites the
        metric by name, and recommends a budget shift. There is no visual tell.
        The reader has no reason to doubt it, and often no way to check it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agentic workflows take the human out of the read path, so the checking
        has to move upstream into the definitions. That is the whole argument
        for the semantic layer, restated by the technology that made it
        urgent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 06 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">06 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The translation problem</p>
      <h2>English is vague on purpose. SQL is not allowed to be.</h2>
      <p>
        The imprecision of human language is a feature. Ambiguity is what
        gives us poetry, irony, diplomacy, and the ability to say something
        useful before we have pinned down every term. Strip it out and you do
        not get a better language, you get a programming language.
      </p>
      <p>
        &ldquo;How many active customers do we have in France?&rdquo; is a
        perfectly good English sentence and an underspecified query. Active
        when, by which signal? A customer at the account level or the seat
        level? France by billing address, by IP, or by the sales territory
        that owns the account? A human analyst resolves those silently, from
        context, and gets it right often enough that nobody notices the
        resolution happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        So expecting plain English to compile into accurate, reproducible
        analytics is asking one language to do a job its grammar was never
        built for. Ask the same question twice and a model may resolve it two
        different ways, both defensible, both producing a different number.
        For a decision you have to stand behind, that is not an answer.
      </p>
      <p class="gnd-note">
        The semantic layer is where the ambiguity gets resolved once, in the
        open, by people who are accountable for the choice. The agent then
        picks from a set of defined terms rather than inventing a reading of
        yours. The question stays in English. The answer comes from something
        deterministic.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 07 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">07 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The quiet side effect</p>
      <h2>Gen AI may be the killer app the semantic layer was waiting for.</h2>
      <p>
        The idea is old. Looker shipped modeled metrics in LookML more than a
        decade ago, and dbt, Cube, and the BI vendors have each carried their
        own YAML conventions for years. Adoption stayed patchy, because a good
        analyst could compensate. They knew which field was a trap, and they
        patched the model in their head on the way to the answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agents cannot patch anything in their heads. Hand one an ambiguous
        field and it will pick a reading and commit to it. Suddenly the
        <code>.yaml</code> file that nobody wanted to maintain is the difference
        between an assistant and a liability, and the conventions are
        converging: the Open Semantic Interchange spec defines an
        <code>ai_context</code> field at model, dataset, field, and metric
        level, and Omni implements it under the same name.
      </p>
      <p>
        OSI marks that field optional. Treat it as required. It is the only
        channel through which your semantic layer tells an agent what it
        actually meant.
      </p>
      <p class="gnd-note">
        Who writes those fields is a governance question rather than a turf
        one. My argument for the split, analyst-defined, engineer-constrained,
        published only on joint sign-off, is written up in
        <a
          href="https://github.com/aleberriz/intelligence-layer/blob/main/semantic-layer/frameworks/ownership-model.md"
          >the semantic layer ownership model</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 06 ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-card">
      <p class="gnd-num">08 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; Before the floors go up</p>
      <h2>Five things to do before anyone says &ldquo;AI first&rdquo; out loud.</h2>
      <ol class="gnd-spec gnd-spec--steps">
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Write down the ten questions</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >The ten the business actually asks every week. Not the hundred it
            might ask one day.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Define every term in them</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >In business language, with the exclusions spelled out, in the
            semantic layer rather than in a wiki page.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Put a name on each definition</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >One person who answers for the meaning, one who answers for the
            math, and no publishing without both.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Let the agent answer the ten</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >Then log every wrong answer against the definition that caused it.
            That log is your pile inspection report.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="sp-q">Ship the narrow thing</span>
          <span class="sp-d"
            >A cottage on rock beats a palace on silt, and it can be extended
            later. The palace can only be evacuated.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ CLOSER ============ -->
    <section class="gnd-close">
      <p class="gnd-num">The job</p>
      <p class="gnd-shout">Drive the piles, then argue about the pool.</p>
      <p>
        The pressure to start above the waterline is real, and it comes with a
        deadline attached. Start below it anyway. The demo you can give in
        three months is the one that still works in twelve, and the work it
        rests on outlives whichever model you are renting this quarter.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ TITLE BLOCK ============ -->
    <footer class="gnd-foot">
      <dl class="gnd-block">
        <div><dt>Note</dt><dd>Gen AI without data</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Field</dt><dd>AI and the semantic layer</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Drawn by</dt><dd>Alejandro Berrizbeitia</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Founding</dt><dd>On rock</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="gnd-foot-h">Go deeper</p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/aleberriz/intelligence-layer/blob/main/semantic-layer/frameworks/ownership-model.md"
          >Semantic layer ownership model</a
        >. Who defines meaning, who validates correctness, and why neither
        should publish alone once an agent is reading the output.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://open-semantic-interchange.org/">Open Semantic Interchange</a>.
        The cross-vendor spec where <code>ai_context</code> lives.
      </p>
      <p class="gnd-back">
        <a href="/field-notes/">← All field notes</a>
        <a href="/">aleberriz.com</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo+Black&family=Archivo:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');

  .gnd {
    --sky: #12303f;
    --deep: #041820;
    --sea: #0a3a47;
    --foam: #7fd8d2;
    --amber: #f6b93b;
    --coral: #ff6f59;
    --body: #cfe0e4;
    --dim: #7d97a1;
    --rule: rgba(127, 216, 210, 0.16);

    min-height: 100vh;
    color: var(--body);
    /* the waterline runs down the whole page, not just the drawing */
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, #0d2532 0 240px, var(--deep) 560px),
      var(--deep);
    font-family: Archivo, Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .gnd-inner {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.25rem 4rem;
  }

  .gnd p { margin: 0 0 1.05rem; }
  .gnd a { color: var(--foam); text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .gnd a:hover { color: var(--amber); }
  .gnd code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--amber);
  }

  .gnd-kicker,
  .gnd-num,
  .gnd-foot-h,
  .gnd-back,
  .gnd-block dt {
    font-family: Archivo, Inter, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  /* ---------- hero ---------- */
  .gnd-hero { padding: 4rem 0 3rem; }
  .gnd-hero h1 {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: 'Archivo Black', Archivo, Inter, sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 9.5vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: #fff;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .gnd-dek {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: #a8c2c9;
    max-width: 38ch;
  }

  /* ---------- the drawing ---------- */
  .gnd-meme { margin: 2.5rem 0 0; }
  .gnd-panels {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 2px;
    background: rgba(127, 216, 210, 0.22);
    border: 2px solid rgba(127, 216, 210, 0.22);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .gnd-panels svg {
    flex: 1 1 210px;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }
  .gnd-meme figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  .g-sky { fill: #1d4257; }
  .g-water { fill: #0a3546; }
  .g-rock { fill: #05141b; }
  .g-bed {
    stroke: #7fd8d2;
    stroke-opacity: 0.45;
    stroke-width: 2;
    fill: none;
  }
  .g-wall { fill: #e8ddc6; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; }
  .g-trim { fill: #c9b998; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; }
  .g-roof { fill: #e2704a; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; }
  .g-pool { fill: #56c2d6; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; }
  .g-win rect { fill: #1d4257; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .g-para { fill: #e2704a; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  .g-thin { fill: none; stroke: #05141b; stroke-width: 2; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .g-crack { fill: none; stroke: #c0392b; stroke-width: 2; stroke-linejoin: round; }
